<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">West Bengal</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">West <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a> is a state in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kolkata">Kolkata</a> as its capital, stretching from the Himalayan foothills at Darjeeling to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bay-of-bengal">Bay of Bengal</a>. It was created at the partition of 1947, when the western, largely <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> part of the Bengal Province joined India and the eastern part became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-pakistan">East Pakistan</a>, later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bangladesh">Bangladesh</a>. It is one of India's most populous states.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/west-bengal.jpg" alt="West Bengal" width="320" height="163" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Shubhankar.sengupta19 · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Howrah_bridge_betwixt_Lights.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>West <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a> is on the eastern bottleneck of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, stretching from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/himalayas">Himalayas</a> in the north to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bay-of-bengal">Bay of Bengal</a> in the south. The Darjeeling Himalayan hill region in the far north is part of the eastern Himalayas. The narrow <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/terai">Terai</a> separates the hills from the plains of North Bengal, which give way southward to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ganges-plain">Ganges</a> delta, and the Sundarbans mangrove forests, part of the world's largest mangrove forest, mark the delta's southern edge.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stone-age">Stone Age</a> tools some 20,000 years old have been found in the state. According to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahabharata">Mahabharata</a> the region was part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vanga">Vanga</a> kingdom. Greek writers around 100 BCE mention a land called Gangaridai at the mouths of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ganges-plain">Ganges</a>. The region belonged to several pan-Indian empires, including those of the Mauryans and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gupta-empire">Guptas</a>, and the citadel of Gauḍa was the capital of the Gauda kingdom, the Pala Empire and the Sena Empire. After the Ghurid conquests led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bakhtiyar-khalji">Bakhtiyar Khalji</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> spread across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a>. Under the Bengal Sultanate the region was a major trading land, and it was absorbed into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughal Empire</a> in 1576.</p><p>The British East India Company defeated the last independent Nawab, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siraj-ud-daulah">Siraj ud-Daulah</a>, at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-plassey">Battle of Plassey</a> in 1757, and after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-buxar">Battle of Buxar</a> in 1764 it won the right to collect revenue in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a>. The Bengal famine of 1770 claimed millions of lives. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kolkata">Calcutta</a>, the company's headquarters, became the capital of British-held India, and from 1912 to 1947 it was the capital of the Bengal Province. The region was a centre of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-independence">Indian independence</a> movement and of the Bengali Renaissance.</p><p>In 1947 the Bengal Legislative Council and Assembly voted to partition Bengal along religious lines. The western part, with a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> majority, became the Indian state of West Bengal; the eastern part went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a> as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-bengal">East Bengal</a>, later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-pakistan">East Pakistan</a>, and became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bangladesh">Bangladesh</a> in 1971. Refugees from the east arrived in large numbers for decades.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>West <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a> is a state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kolkata">Kolkata</a> as its capital and largest city. Its population was 91,347,736 at the 2011 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a>. Bengali and English are the official languages, and the main ethnic group is the Bengalis. Its cultural heritage includes the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charyapada">Charyapada</a>, Baul music, the songs of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rabindranath-tagore">Rabindranath Tagore</a> and the Bengal School of Art, and the state has three World Heritage sites.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “West Bengal” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>A 6th-century manuscript from <b>West Bengal</b> presents the epic without the Bala and Uttara Kandas.</blockquote><a href="/work/ramayana">Ramayana · Survival &amp; Rediscovery</a></li><li><blockquote>The division ran along communal lines, with a Muslim majority in East Bengal and Assam and a Hindu majority in <b>West Bengal</b>, and it is claimed to have been politically motivated.</blockquote><a href="/songs/amar-sonar-bangla">Amar Sonar Bangla</a></li><li><blockquote>On 20 July 1905 the British colonial government split the Bengal Presidency in two along communal lines, into East Bengal and Assam, with a Muslim majority, and <b>West Bengal</b>, with a Hindu majority.</blockquote><a href="/songs/dhana-dhanya-pushpa-bhara">Dhana Dhanya Pushpa Bhara</a></li><li><blockquote>At its founding its catchment area stretched from Kabul to Myanmar; today its jurisdiction covers a few districts of <b>West Bengal</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/january-24">24 January in history</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1947 Bengal was partitioned again, <b>West Bengal</b> joining the Dominion of India and East Bengal the Dominion of Pakistan, later becoming Bangladesh.</blockquote><a href="/state/bengal-presidency">Bengal Presidency</a></li><li><blockquote>Maharaja Jagaddipendra Narayan signed a merger agreement with India in August 1949, and Cooch Behar became a district of <b>West Bengal</b> in January 1950.</blockquote><a href="/state/cooch-behar-state">Cooch Behar State</a></li></ul></section>
